description:
crate-jmap-chat-server/tests/integration.rs contains 333 occurrences of the bare string literal `"a1"` (the account id) and ~250 occurrences of placeholder ids `"c0"|"c1"|"s0"|"s1"|"e0"|"m0"|"b0"|"i0"`. Example, integration.rs:174-178:

    json!({
        "accountId": "a1",
        "create": {
            "c0": { "kind": "group", "name": "My Group" }
        }
    }),

The newer sibling test files all use named consts consistently:
- role_member_apply.rs:29 `const ACCOUNT_ID: &str = "a1"` (one bare "a1" in the file, namely the const declaration)
- channel_category_apply.rs:40 const declarations
- space_set_kitchen_sink.rs:38-40
- identity_writes.rs:42-44
- space_metadata_apply.rs

Idiomatic Rust: hoist `ACCOUNT_ID`, `SPACE_ID`, `CHAT_ID_C0`, etc. into tests/common/mod.rs and `use common::*;` them. The hex literal `"a1"` is meaningless to a reader skimming the file; `ACCOUNT_ID` self-documents.

Engineering basis: inconsistency with the rest of the test suite. The oldest test file (integration.rs) was written before the convention was settled; the newer files (role_member_apply, channel_category_apply, space_set_kitchen_sink, identity_writes, space_metadata_apply) all follow the const-named convention. integration.rs has not been retrofitted. When the next contributor adds a multi-account test and decides to call the second account `"a2"`, the bare `"a1"` everywhere obscures whether each call site is intentionally account-1 or just an artifact of copy-paste. This is a textbook test-readability finding with strong sibling-file precedent inside the same crate.

Filed at P3 not P2 because the fix is mechanical (find-replace) and the bug-cost is bounded to readability, not correctness.
